  "rationale": "The added columns supply an abundance of candidate treatments -- actionable product attributes (taste, flavor intensity, price/value, freshness, expectation mismatch) whose effect on the score can be attributed, so requirement (a) is clearly met. Requirement (b), genuine confounders that are common causes of BOTH treatment and outcome and surfaced from the data rather than the query, is only weakly met. Consumer_segment is the sole plausible confounder (segment shapes both which attributes matter/are experienced and scoring behavior) and it was not named in the query, showing some real capability. However, it is arguably as much an effect-modifier as a classic common cause, and repeat_purchase_intent is a downstream mediator/outcome proxy mislabelled as a driver, not a confounder. No design variables (e.g., verified-purchase status, review time period, product category, reviewer helpfulness/experience) that would classically confound attribute-score attribution were surfaced. Overall: strong on treatments, thin and only moderately valid on confounders."
